.\" Man page for ax25common.conf
.\"
.TH AX25COMMON.CONF 5 "29 Sep 2026" "ax25-apps" "Linux System Administration"
.SH NAME
ax25common.conf \- shared loop port configuration of ax25netd and ax25tcpd
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B ax25common.conf
configures the loop port of
.BR ax25netd (8).
The daemon itself and every local client of that loop port read this
file, so the endpoint a client connects to can never drift away from the
endpoint the daemon listens on.  Each line is one directive:
.PP
.RS
.B loop socket
.I path
.br
.B loop socket
.B no
.br
.B loop tcp
.I port
.br
.B loop tcp
.B yes\|no
.br
.B loop group
.I name
.br
.B loop group
.I gid
.br
.B loop group
.B all\|default
.br
.B loop mode
.I octal
.RE
.PP
.B loop socket
gives the unix domain socket of the loop port the path
.IR path .
The default is
.IR /var/run/ax25/sockets/ax25netd.sock ,
and the directory is created if it is missing.  The value
.B no
(or
.B 0
) switches the socket off, which leaves the TCP listener of
.B loop tcp
as the only way in.
.PP
.B loop group
decides who may connect to that socket, and with it which mode the
socket file gets, since connecting to a unix socket requires write
access to it.  The value
.B all
(every local account) gives the socket mode
.BR 0666 ,
and is the default.  A group
.I name
or numeric
.I gid
gives
.B 0660
to that group and the daemon run user.  The conventional group for
AX.25 users is
.B hams
(see axspawn.conf).
.B loop group default
means the daemon run user and its primary group, i.e.
.B 0660
without naming a group.  Note that a socket at
.B 0666
lets every local account onto the loop port, which is normally the
point: these are the local tools of one station.  It does not reach
off the host - that is what the directory mode below is for - and
.B loop group
narrows it if a host has accounts that should not talk AX.25.
.PP
.B loop mode
sets the mode of the directory holding the socket, as octal.  The
default is
.BR 1775 :
world readable and traversable, so tools running as another user can
find the socket, and the sticky bit, so that one local account cannot
rename or remove a socket file belonging to another.  0750 is the
tighter choice if only one group of local users ever connects.
.PP
.B loop tcp
enables a TCP listener of the loop port on
.IR port .
The default is
.BR "no" ,
because a TCP loop port is reachable by everything that can open a TCP
connection to the host and nothing authenticates its peers, whereas the
local socket is not exposed that way.  If you switch it on, prefer
.B loop socket no
with it, or the same service answers twice.
.B loop tcp yes
and
.B loop tcp no
switch the listener without changing the port; switched on without a
port it listens on
.BR 8100 .
.PP
A missing file is not an error and leaves the defaults in place: unix
socket at
.IR /var/run/ax25/sockets/ax25netd.sock ,
no TCP listener, socket group
.BR all ,
directory mode
.BR 1775 .
.PP
Blank lines and lines beginning with
.B #
are ignored.
.SH "THE LOCAL CLIENTS"
Every local client of the loop port reads this file, so a change here
reaches all of them at once:
.TP
.B ax25netd (8)
the server; listens where this file says
.TP
.B ax25tcpd (8)
connects to the loop port, and takes its
.B target
from here when
.B ax25tcpd.conf
names none
.TP
.BR listen (8)
the generic listener behind
.BR ax25tcpd (8)
.TP
the AGWPE shim in libax25, and so every program using it
The WAMPES nodes are named separately, in
.BR wampes.conf (5),
and reach the same upstreams by their own path.
.SH FILES
.I @@@AX25_SYSCONFDIR@@@/ax25common.conf
